    "content": "The last time humans went to the moon was during NASA's Apollo 17 mission, which landed on December 11, 1972, and returned to Earth on December 19, 1972. Astronauts Eugene Cernan and Harrison Schmitt spent over three days on the lunar surface, marking the final crewed lunar landing to date. Mission Commander: Eugene Cernan (the last person to walk on the moon). Lunar Module Pilot: Harrison Schmitt (the first trained geologist to visit the moon). Command Module Pilot: Ronald Evans. Landing Site: Taurus-Littrow valley. While several uncrewed missions have landed on the moon since, Apollo 17 remains the last time humans walked on its surface. NASA is currently working to return humans to the moon through the Artemis program, with the Artemis II mission slated to circle the moon in the near future and Artemis III planned for a landing. Artemis II mission Artemis III",
